= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

A <sequence> $(a_n)$ of <real numbers> is a <convergent sequence> with <limit of a sequence> $\ell$ when
$$
\boxed{\forall\varepsilon>0\ \exists N\in\mathbb N\ \forall n\geq N:\quad |a_n-\ell|<\varepsilon.}
$$
Divergence to positive <infinity> means
$$
\boxed{\forall M\in\mathbb R\ \exists N\in\mathbb N\ \forall n\geq N:\quad a_n>M.}
$$
In both definitions the same $N$ must control every later term. The second condition is not convergence to a <real number>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Suppose $a_n\to\ell$. With tolerance $1$, choose $N$ such that $|a_n-\ell|<1$ whenever $n\geq N$. The <triangle inequality> gives $|a_n|\leq|a_n-\ell|+|\ell|<1+|\ell|$ for these terms. Set
$$
\boxed{B=1+|\ell|+\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}|a_j|.}
$$
An empty sum is zero. This finite $B$ satisfies $|a_n|\leq B$ for every $n$, so every <convergent sequence> is a <bounded sequence>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Put $u=c^{-1}-1>0$, so $c=(1+u)^{-1}$. By <Bernoulli's inequality>, $(1+u)^n\geq1+nu$, and hence
$$
\boxed{0<c^n\leq\frac1{1+nu}\longrightarrow0.}
$$
More explicitly, for $\varepsilon>0$ choose an <integer> $N>1/(u\varepsilon)$. For $n\geq N$, the right-hand side is less than $\varepsilon$. Thus the <geometric sequence> has <limit of a sequence> zero, either directly by the definition or by the <squeeze theorem>.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

Given $\varepsilon>0$, the two assumed <limits of sequences> supply thresholds $N_1,N_2$ such that $|a_n-\ell|<\varepsilon$ for $n\geq N_1$ and $|c_n-\ell|<\varepsilon$ for $n\geq N_2$. For $n\geq\max\{N_1,N_2\}$,
$$
\ell-\varepsilon<a_n\leq b_n\leq c_n<\ell+\varepsilon.
$$
Thus $|b_n-\ell|<\varepsilon$ eventually, proving the <squeeze theorem>:
$$
\boxed{b_n\longrightarrow\ell.}
$$

