= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**False.** Take the positive <sequence> $\boxed{a_n=n}$. Then
$$
\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\frac{n+1}{n}=1+\frac1n\longrightarrow1,
$$
but $a_n\to+\infty$, so it has no finite <limit of a sequence>. Successive ratios close to one do not prevent cumulative growth.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**False.** Take the positive <geometric sequence> $\boxed{a_n=2^{-n}}$. It tends to zero, while
$$
\boxed{\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\frac12\not\longrightarrow1.}
$$
A positive <limit of a sequence> would make the claim true since $a_{n+1}/a_n$ would then tend to $\ell/\ell=1$, but positivity of each individual term does not exclude a zero <limit of a sequence>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

**True.** Since $a_n^n\to2$, there is $N_0$ such that $1<a_n^n<3$ for $n\geq N_0$. Positivity then gives $a_n>1$ on this tail.

Fix $\varepsilon>0$. If $a_n\geq1+\varepsilon$, <Bernoulli's inequality> gives
$$
a_n^n\geq(1+\varepsilon)^n\geq1+n\varepsilon.
$$
For $n>2/\varepsilon$, this contradicts $a_n^n<3$. Therefore eventually $1<a_n<1+\varepsilon$, proving
$$
\boxed{a_n\longrightarrow1.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

**True.** Choose $N$ such that $\ell/2<a_n<3\ell/2$ for every $n\geq N$. All the finitely many preceding terms are also positive. Define the positive <minimum> and finite <maximum>
$$
\eta=\min\bigl(\{\ell/2\}\cup\{a_j:1\leq j<N\}\bigr)>0,
\qquad
B=\max\bigl(\{3\ell/2\}\cup\{a_j:1\leq j<N\}\bigr)<\infty.
$$
Every term lies between $\eta$ and $B$, so every ratio is at most $B/\eta$. Consequently the required <supremum> satisfies
$$
\boxed{\sup\{a_n/a_m:m,n\in\mathbb N\}\leq B/\eta<\infty.}
$$

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}
{c}

**False.** Choose the positive <sequence>
$$
\boxed{a_n=1+\frac1{\sqrt n}.}
$$
It has <limit of a sequence> one, but <Bernoulli's inequality> gives
$$
a_n^n=\left(1+\frac1{\sqrt n}\right)^n\geq1+\sqrt n\longrightarrow+\infty.
$$
Thus $(a_n^n)$ is not a <bounded sequence>.

