= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For a <series> of <complex numbers>, form the <partial sums> $S_N=\sum_{n=1}^Na_n$. The <series> converges when these <partial sums> have a finite <limit of a sequence> $S\in\mathbb C$:
$$
\boxed{\forall\varepsilon>0\ \exists N_0\ \forall N\geq N_0:\quad |S_N-S|<\varepsilon.}
$$
The <series> has <absolute convergence> when $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}|a_n|$ converges as a <series> of nonnegative <real numbers>. A <series> beginning at $n=0$ uses the same definitions with that starting index.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $T_N=\sum_{n=1}^N|a_n|$ and $S_N=\sum_{n=1}^Na_n$. If $(T_N)$ converges, it is a <Cauchy sequence>. Thus, for every $\varepsilon>0$, some $N$ makes
$$
\left|S_q-S_p\right|=\left|\sum_{n=p+1}^qa_n\right|\leq\sum_{n=p+1}^q|a_n|=T_q-T_p<\varepsilon
\qquad(q>p\geq N),
$$
where the <triangle inequality> gives the bound. Therefore $(S_N)$ is a <Cauchy sequence> in the <complex numbers>. These form a <complete metric space>, so $(S_N)$ has a finite <limit of a sequence>. Equivalently, apply the <Cauchy criterion for series>. Hence **<absolute convergence> implies convergence.**

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The finite <geometric series> identity, obtained by subtracting $t$ times the sum from the sum itself, gives
$$
S_N=\sum_{n=0}^Nt^n=\frac{1-t^{N+1}}{1-t}.
$$
For $0<t<1$, the <geometric sequence> $t^{N+1}$ tends to zero by Question 2(a)(iii). Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}t^n=\frac1{1-t}.}
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <radius of convergence> of a <power series> $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}a_nz^n$ is the number $R\in[0,\infty]$ such that the <series> has <absolute convergence> for $|z|<R$ and diverges for $|z|>R$. Equivalently,
$$
\boxed{R=\sup\left\{r\geq0:\ \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}|a_n|r^n<\infty\right\}.}
$$
Use $r^0=1$, including at $r=0$. The <supremum> may be $+\infty$. There is no general conclusion at $|z|=R$: boundary convergence depends on the <coefficients>. If $R=0$, only the centre is guaranteed; if $R=\infty$, every finite $z$ lies inside the <disk of convergence>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The <comparison test for series> states that, for $0\leq u_n\leq v_n$ eventually, convergence of $\sum v_n$ implies convergence of $\sum u_n$. Equivalently, divergence of $\sum u_n$ implies divergence of $\sum v_n$.

For $|z|<1$, the coefficient bound gives
$$
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}|a_nz^n|\leq M\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}|z|^n<\infty
$$
by the <geometric series> formula and the <comparison test for series>. The single term $a_0$ does not affect convergence. For $|z|\geq1$,
$$
|a_nz^n|>m|z|^n\geq m>0\qquad(n\geq1),
$$
so the terms do not tend to zero. The fact that <terms of a convergent series tend to zero> excludes convergence there. Thus
$$
\boxed{R=1.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

If $z_0=0$, there is no $z$ with $|z|<|z_0|$, so the claim is vacuous. Suppose $z_0\ne0$. Convergence of $\sum a_nz_0^n$ implies $a_nz_0^n\to0$, because <terms of a convergent series tend to zero>. In particular this is a <bounded sequence>, so $|a_nz_0^n|\leq C$ for some finite $C$ and every $n$.

For $|z|<|z_0|$, put $q=|z|/|z_0|<1$. Then
$$
|a_nz^n|=|a_nz_0^n|q^n\leq Cq^n.
$$
The <geometric series> and <comparison test for series> prove
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}|a_nz^n|<\infty.}
$$
Thus convergence at one point gives <absolute convergence> at every point strictly closer to the centre.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

All three examples below have positive <integer> <coefficients>, so they work under either convention for whether $0\in\mathbb N$.

For $R=1$, take $\boxed{a_n=1}$. This is the <geometric series>: it converges for $|z|<1$, and its terms fail to tend to zero for $|z|\geq1$.

For $R=0$, take $\boxed{a_n=n!}$, using the <factorial> and $0!=1$. For every fixed $z\ne0$, the <absolute values> of successive terms have ratio $(n+1)|z|$. This eventually exceeds two, so the terms grow without bound and the <series> cannot converge. At $z=0$ it is finite, giving <radius of convergence> zero.

For $R=1/\sqrt2$, take
$$
\boxed{a_n=2^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor},}
$$
using the <floor function>. The <coefficients> satisfy
$$
\frac{(\sqrt2)^n}{\sqrt2}\leq a_n\leq(\sqrt2)^n.
$$
The upper bound and the <comparison test for series> give <absolute convergence> when $\sqrt2|z|<1$. The lower bound prevents the terms tending to zero when $\sqrt2|z|\geq1$. Hence the <radius of convergence> is exactly $1/\sqrt2$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Use the <ceiling function> to choose positive <integer> <coefficients>
$$
\boxed{a_n=\lceil\alpha^{-n}\rceil\qquad(n\geq0).}
$$
Because $0<\alpha<1$, we have $\alpha^{-n}\geq1$ and
$$
\alpha^{-n}\leq a_n<\alpha^{-n}+1\leq2\alpha^{-n}.
$$
For $|z|<\alpha$, the <comparison test for series> with the <geometric series> gives
$$
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}|a_nz^n|\leq2\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\left(\frac{|z|}{\alpha}\right)^n<\infty.
$$
For $|z|\geq\alpha$, we instead have $|a_nz^n|\geq(|z|/\alpha)^n\geq1$, so the terms do not tend to zero. Thus **the <power series> has <radius of convergence> exactly $\alpha$.**

